# E99 research swarm pilot plan

**Status: prepared, not launched.** This document proposes a bounded evaluation of
research coordination and verification. No pilot sessions or outside submissions
were started for this plan.

Prepared against `main` commit
`ab6dd3336f9f8d440f2bada76edb32153391cddc`, state version
`2026-10-10.audit.234`. These values are a preparation snapshot, not a permanent
release pin. Refresh both from fetched `origin/main` before any pilot session.

## Purpose and scope

Evaluate whether 10–20 isolated agent sessions can produce reviewable, reproducible
E99 research with useful overlap control and a manageable independent-review load.
The pilot is a process evaluation. It is not a public launch, a claim that the
research archive is ready for unrestricted publication, or an attempt to solve E99
within a fixed time window.

Use 12 sessions in three waves of four. Each session receives one bounded task from
the E99 task catalogue once that catalogue is merged. The catalogue and selection
instructions are expected from the Agent 6 deliverable; if it is not available, do
not invent a task or call an endpoint unexplored. Use the current authority files to
write a task card with the same fields first. The overlap and review process should
follow the Agent 7 governance deliverable when available; until then use the
existing contribution and promotion policies.

## Entry gates

Before starting a wave, the pilot owner should:

1. Fetch `origin/main`; record its exact SHA and `docs/state/theorem-state.yaml`
   `state_version`. Read `docs/STATE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, and the active route map and
   prompt for every selected task.
2. Install the declared verifier dependencies and run the same baseline checks as
   CI. From the repository root:

   ```sh
   python3 -m pip install --disable-pip-version-check -r tools/requirements-theorem-state.txt
   python3 -m unittest tools/test_check_theorem_state.py tools/test_check_theorem_state_pr.py tools/test_check_active_route_summaries.py tools/test_run_theorem_certificates.py
   python3 tools/check_theorem_state.py
   python3 tools/check_active_route_summaries.py
   python3 tools/check_theorem_state_pr.py
   python3 tools/run_theorem_certificates.py
   git diff --check
   ```

   Record environment and any pre-existing failures before assigning work.
3. Review open PRs and active task claims for exact-question overlap. Normalize the
   proposed statement, hypotheses, source configuration, and intended conclusion;
   similar keywords alone do not establish duplicate work.
4. Confirm each task has a bounded question, canonical dependencies, relevant prior
   obstructions, a feasible first check, expected evidence, and a stopping condition.
5. Confirm each session has an isolated checkout and a dedicated branch. Do not share
   credentials, use privileged workflow tokens, or let agents edit canonical state.

Do not begin if the authoritative state changed after task preparation. Re-read the
affected route, compare the dependency graph, and either update the card or retire it.

## Allocation across 12 sessions

Assign three sessions to each principal route and three cross-cutting sessions:

| Sessions | Assignment mix | Intent |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1–3 | Target A, Target B, Target C-II | One distinct current obligation per route, selected from the live catalogue. |
| 4 | Independent audit or exact computation/regression | Cross-check a scoped claim or falsify a finite statement. |
| 5–7 | Target A, Target B, Target C-II | Separate task cards with nonoverlapping claims or dependencies. |
| 8 | Different-approach or literature task | Assess a method against the exact global quantifiers. |
| 9–11 | Target A, Target B, Target C-II | Further route coverage; prioritize routes with fewer active claims. |
| 12 | Independent audit or exact computation/regression | Replicate one earlier task or produce a bounded negative result. |

Run three waves. Wave 1 uses sessions 1–4, Wave 2 uses 5–8, and Wave 3 uses 9–12.
After each wave, update the open-task and overlap record before assigning the next
wave. Reserve one cross-cutting task in each wave so the pilot does not collapse into
one route's local terminology. One of the 12 assignments may be an intentional
independent replication of an earlier task; mark that pair as planned replication,
not accidental duplication.

Within each wave, weight selection toward routes and task types with fewer active
claims. Do not give a task higher weight because its hoped-for conclusion would
advance the frontier. A useful exact counterexample, corrected scope, reproducible
negative result, or partial proof audit counts as a successful research outcome.

## Session contract and evidence package

Every session begins from its recorded base SHA and state version and checks open
PRs before work. Its handoff should include:

- task-card identifier and exact question;
- base SHA, state version, branch, and changed-file list;
- precise assumptions and canonical theorem IDs used;
- prior obstruction or regression checked;
- proof or computation, including exact arithmetic and reproducible commands where
  computation is material;
- result status: proved candidate awaiting promotion, unreviewed candidate,
  audited-working, conditional, regression, or rejected;
- what the evidence does not establish, including any missing global-minimizer,
  unbounded-cardinality, source-coembedding, or finite-motion implication;
- overlap found, files another task depends on, and an explicit stopping condition.

Exploratory work may be submitted as a report or focused PR without changing the
theorem manifest. An agent must not edit `docs/state/theorem-state.yaml`, generated
theorem views, or active route summaries to declare its own result canonical. A
maintainer review and the repository's promotion transaction are required for any
frontier change.

## CI and concurrent-work expectations

Run the always-on state and tooling checks for every PR. The change-aware certificate
runner skips unrelated certificates only when the theorem manifest, active routes,
canonical proof sources, verification tooling, workflows, and canonical certificates
are untouched. A change to those load-bearing inputs must run the full registered
certificate suite. A failed base diff must fail closed to the full suite. The push to
`main` also runs every registered certificate. The historical Target C workflows
remain scoped, separately named verifier workflows and are not substitutes for the
theorem-state check. A passing certificate replay confirms its registered executable
check on the recorded inputs; it does not independently validate the associated
analytic proof.

Reviewers should compare exact statements and dependencies, keep concurrent branches
separate, and preserve failed or superseded attempts. Merge order must not make a
merged, newer, or larger-numbered report canonical by implication. Do not run this
pilot through `pull_request_target` or any workflow that gives untrusted PR code
write-capable credentials.

## Measures

Record outcomes by task, wave, route, and reviewer. Do not rank contributors from raw
counts.

| Measure | Counting rule |
| --- | --- |
| Substantive findings | Proof candidates with a complete stated scope, independently checked exact regressions, or useful negative results with reproducible evidence. Summaries alone do not count. |
| Duplicate effort | Unplanned task pairs that attack the same exact statement under materially identical hypotheses. Track planned replications separately. |
| Independent verification | Findings checked by someone other than the submitting session, with a recorded disposition and any scope correction. |
| Maintainer review burden | Active reviewer time per handoff, separated into mathematical review, computational replay, and repository integration. |
| Reproducibility | Fraction of computational packages that run from a clean checkout with the recorded commands and pinned inputs. Mathematical proof checking is recorded separately. |
| Invalid or overstated submissions | Handoffs whose proof, computation, dependencies, quantifiers, or status claim fails review. Preserve the reason and resulting correction. |
| Useful negative results | Exact counterexamples, failed hypotheses, or bounded no-go results that prevent repetition or narrow a route. Record the scope and how another task can reuse them. |

Proposed continuation gates for the 12-session pilot are: at least 9 complete
handoffs; at least 4 independently useful findings; at least 75% of submitted
computational packages reproducible; no more than 3 unplanned duplicate task pairs;
no unresolved credential or workflow-safety incident; and a median review burden of
no more than 2 reviewer-hours per handoff. Report the numerator, denominator, and
unreviewed remainder for every measure. These are proposed operating thresholds,
not observed results or a promise that agent research will be mathematically valid.

Pause new assignments if a task relies on a stale state, a security issue is
unresolved, exact-question overlap is causing repeated work, or the review backlog
exceeds the available independent reviewers. Resume only after the task catalogue,
overlap map, or review capacity has been corrected.

## Pilot closeout

After Wave 3, publish an internal pilot report that records the base and final state
versions, session and task counts, all seven measures, representative accepted and
rejected handoffs, review dispositions, CI time and failures, and specific process
changes proposed. Keep every mathematical result at its reviewed lifecycle status.
Do not infer public-release readiness or theorem progress from the pilot metrics.

## Not evaluated here

This plan does not launch sessions, verify the repository for public release, select
a license, change repository visibility, grant collaborator access, or claim that
CI has completed a swarm-scale load test. Those require separate owner decisions and
evidence.

## Design references

The process design draws on the weighted task portfolio, bounded task card, explicit
overlap check, stopping criteria, reproducible handoff, and preserved credit in
[CrocSwap's autonomous research guide](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/blob/main/autoresearch.md),
[contribution and independent-review guide](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md),
and [contributor record](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/blob/main/CONTRIBUTORS.md).
The E99 authority files and lifecycle categories continue to govern every
mathematical claim and promotion decision.

## Integrated coordination and verification

Use the [issue-based claim workflow](../COORDINATION.md) and selector `--live-overlap`
before allocating a session. A seeded suggestion is not a claim. Discussions are
currently disabled and no setting change is required. Use the [local full-verification
command](CI.md) while hosted checks are quota-blocked. The [release dependency plan](RELEASE_PREPARATION.md)
is required before offering a curated task bundle. This integration has not launched
any session or measured outside-contributor throughput; the numerical pilot targets
above remain proposed acceptance criteria.
